<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Utah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Utah is a state in the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with its capital at Salt Lake City. Settled from 1847 by members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints">Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a>, it was organised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah-territory">Utah Territory</a> in 1850 after the Mormons' proposed State of Deseret was rejected. Conflict with the federal government over polygamy delayed statehood until 1896, and the church remains central to its life.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/utah.jpg" alt="Utah" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The State of Utah · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Utah.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Utah is a landlocked state in the Mountain West and one of the Four Corners states, sharing a border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a>, and also bordering Wyoming, Idaho and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a>. It lies at the convergence of three geological regions: the Rocky Mountains, the Great Basin and the Colorado Plateau. Most of the western half lies in the Great Basin. The Wasatch Range runs down the middle of the northern third, and the Uinta Mountains in the northeast hold Kings Peak, the highest point. At the western base of the Wasatch Range lies the Wasatch Front, a series of valleys and basins stretching from Brigham City to Nephi, where about 75 per cent of the population lives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Ancestral Puebloans and the Fremont people lived in Utah for thousands of years before Europeans arrived; the Fremont built houses of straw and disappeared from the region around the 15th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a> settled in the region around the 18th century, and in the mid-18th century the Goshute, Paiute, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshone">Shoshone</a> and Ute also settled there. The name Utah comes from the Spanish Yuta, recorded from the 17th century and borrowed from the name of the Ute, a people whose name for themselves is Núuchi-u. It is commonly said to mean people of the mountains.</p><p>Francisco Vázquez de Coronado explored southern Utah in 1540 while looking for Cíbola. A group led by two Catholic priests, the Domínguez-Escalante expedition, left Santa Fe in 1776 hoping to find a route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and travelled as far north as Utah Lake. In 1821, the year <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> became independent, the region became part of its territory of Alta California.</p><p>Many early settlers, even under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, among them Mormons fleeing persecution. In 1848, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican-American War</a>, the region was annexed by the United States and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah-territory">Utah Territory</a>. Disputes between the Mormon community and Congress delayed statehood until 1896, when, after Utah agreed to outlaw polygamy, it was admitted as the 45th state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital is Salt Lake City, on the Wasatch Front. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 3,271,616 people. Slightly over half of Utahns are Mormons, mostly members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints">Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</a>, which has its world headquarters in Salt Lake City, and Utah is the only state where a majority belongs to a single church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Utah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1944 fourteen Comanche code talkers landed at <b>Utah</b> Beach with the US 4th Infantry Division, using their own language to pass army messages in Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/comancheria">Comancheria · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Utah-based LDS Church banned it in 1890, and statehood for <b>Utah</b> soon followed.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mormonism">Mormonism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>They lived across the Colorado Plateau around the Four Corners, where Arizona, New Mexico, Colorado and <b>Utah</b> meet, and archaeologists distinguish regional traditions such as Chaco in north-western New Mexico, the Northern San Juan or Mesa Verde region of south-western Colorado and south-eastern Utah, and Kayenta in north-eastern…</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Mormons from <b>Utah</b> also investigated it, for whom the concepts of the Indian prophet were familiar and often accepted.</blockquote><a href="/movement/ghost-dance">Ghost Dance · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Utah</b> Phillips, Pete Seeger and John Darnielle are among those who have performed it, and it is sung in the 2014 film Pride, about the London group Lesbians and Gays Support the Miners in the 1984–1985 UK miners' strike.</blockquote><a href="/songs/solidarity-forever">Solidarity Forever</a></li><li><blockquote>Harry McClintock is credited as the first to sing it in public, and Woody Guthrie, Pete Seeger and <b>Utah</b> Phillips sang it later.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-preacher-and-the-slave">The Preacher and the Slave</a></li></ul></section>
